[SPEAKER_02]: But all of those ways, all of those movements, are possible only if the intention has arisen beforehand.

362
00:28:37,709 --> 00:28:40,790
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is no intention, you can't conceive a movement of that intention.

363
00:28:43,011 --> 00:28:50,774
[SPEAKER_02]: And there's many... there's many intentions... You can say there is an infinite amount of movement of the given arisen intention.

364
00:28:51,014 --> 00:28:52,975
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not... you recognize...

365
00:28:53,933 --> 00:29:19,055
[SPEAKER_02]: If the intention is the necessary cause, the phenomenon of intention, the presence of intentionality, is a necessary cause for any particular intention to be manifested, you recognize that any particular intention that you engage in, intentional intention, volition, whatever else, it's completely subordinate, completely dependent upon, completely determined by the phenomenon of intention being present.

366
00:29:21,200 --> 00:29:24,161
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you discern that, you will go, oh, well, you have absolutely no say.

367
00:29:24,201 --> 00:29:26,662
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can't intend the phenomenon of intention.

368
00:29:27,783 --> 00:29:28,083
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

369
00:29:29,003 --> 00:29:32,985
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, you can't ever even conceive it to be yours.

370
00:29:34,345 --> 00:29:45,410
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, because of upon that very thing that is inconceivable to be yours, your whole volition, your whole sense of self, your whole intentionality, stands upon, and it's completely determined by, and inseparable from.

371
00:29:46,970 --> 00:29:50,132
[SPEAKER_02]: So, by seeing that, then you're like, no matter how hard I will something,

372
00:29:51,665 --> 00:29:54,887
[SPEAKER_02]: It cannot... it's inconceivable to be taken as mine.

373
00:29:54,947 --> 00:29:55,627
[SPEAKER_02]: It's impossible.

374
00:29:56,388 --> 00:29:59,750
[SPEAKER_02]: By fully discerning that impossibility, you'll stop taking it as yours.

375
00:30:00,210 --> 00:30:02,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's... you're making a choice.

376
00:30:02,772 --> 00:30:06,494
[SPEAKER_00]: That's very much as we used to think you are.

377
00:30:06,514 --> 00:30:08,295
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where your sense of self sits, yeah.

378
00:30:08,375 --> 00:30:09,716
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that you're able to choose.

379
00:30:09,976 --> 00:30:10,636
[SPEAKER_00]: I decide.

380
00:30:10,777 --> 00:30:12,938
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's not... like, again, it's such a point.

381
00:30:12,958 --> 00:30:14,719
[SPEAKER_02]: You see, even Bhattacharyya can get a hint of that.

382
00:30:16,202 --> 00:30:23,284
[SPEAKER_02]: You might be able to choose, but when you're not able to not choose, because even when you say, I'm not choosing, you've just chosen that.

383
00:30:23,684 --> 00:30:23,944
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

384
00:30:24,084 --> 00:30:25,704
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're completely... What?

385
00:30:25,864 --> 00:30:26,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Not to choose.

386
00:30:26,845 --> 00:30:27,045
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

387
00:30:27,125 --> 00:30:28,525
[SPEAKER_02]: If so, I'm refraining from choice.

388
00:30:28,545 --> 00:30:30,005
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you just chose to not choose.

389
00:30:30,266 --> 00:30:31,106
[SPEAKER_02]: There's another choice.

390
00:30:31,886 --> 00:30:34,487
[SPEAKER_02]: There's always a possibility of your choice to refrain from choice.

391
00:30:35,267 --> 00:30:36,167
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you always end up...

392
00:30:36,347 --> 00:30:36,967
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why?

393
00:30:37,007 --> 00:30:37,707
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is exactly?

394
00:30:37,747 --> 00:30:38,648
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the core of it.

395
00:30:38,788 --> 00:30:39,788
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact that you can't

396
00:30:41,117 --> 00:30:42,939
[SPEAKER_02]: Choose your way out of choice.

397
00:30:43,320 --> 00:30:45,162
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't intentionally stop intending.

398
00:30:46,043 --> 00:30:51,188
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's exactly, like, why you're condemned to kamma and then the eventual results of it.

399
00:30:51,208 --> 00:30:52,990
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't stop performing kamma.

400
00:30:53,851 --> 00:31:00,419
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't stop performing action, which means you can't stop experiencing results, no matter how far ahead they are.

401
00:31:01,912 --> 00:31:04,674
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why every intention, every action is intention.

402
00:31:05,255 --> 00:31:18,886
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you are condemned to such intentions that you know escape from, means, oh, result of it will be, it will result in action eventually, which means, oh, whatever then results of those actions are, will fall onto me, even I have no say over that.

403
00:31:19,647 --> 00:31:23,050
[SPEAKER_02]: And you never had say in it from the beginning, but by not knowing that,

404
00:31:24,148 --> 00:31:32,133
[SPEAKER_02]: You just kept doing it, and kept appropriating it, which means whatever came as a result of it, later on, was also falling on your end.

405
00:31:37,997 --> 00:31:47,943
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all acts have results, but for an arahant, they don't fall onto anybody.

406
00:31:47,963 --> 00:31:49,584
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, every action is intention.

407
00:31:50,723 --> 00:31:56,666
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, if you fully discern intentions, none of those intentions will be yours.

408
00:31:56,686 --> 00:32:02,370
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, anything that those intentions result in, any acts done on current, won't be actually act.

409
00:32:02,390 --> 00:32:12,836
[SPEAKER_02]: Because act, in the sense of kamma, is when the intention is appropriated and that it brought you to result in committing an action of a kind, which means then the results of those actions will also be yours.

410
00:32:14,331 --> 00:32:15,552
[SPEAKER_00]: But there will still be results.

411
00:32:15,652 --> 00:32:19,614
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't, like, you can say, oh, stop performing actions, because action, kamma, is bad.

412
00:32:20,474 --> 00:32:33,321
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you don't address, if you don't overcome, if you stop appropriate, if you don't stop appropriating your intention, means you're not really abstaining from any action, because from that point of view, even not performing an action is a form of action.

413
00:32:34,222 --> 00:32:35,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you find it.

414
00:32:35,503 --> 00:32:39,645
[SPEAKER_02]: And sooner or later, you will be, you'll do something that will be resulting in one way or the other.

415
00:32:40,719 --> 00:32:44,701
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't see... can you see that intention directly?

416
00:32:44,761 --> 00:32:45,342
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you can't.

417
00:32:45,402 --> 00:32:45,802
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

418
00:32:46,142 --> 00:32:47,403
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you can see the movement.

419
00:32:47,523 --> 00:32:53,907
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if you're expecting to stop the intention, to stop the movement in order to examine it, means you're already looking at the wrong place.

420
00:32:55,088 --> 00:32:55,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can't have any...

421
00:32:55,908 --> 00:33:02,432
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact that there is a movement, means there is the phenomenon of movement that is still.

422
00:33:03,393 --> 00:33:08,055
[SPEAKER_02]: Basically, literally, the trajectory you move has to be given beforehand.

423
00:33:08,175 --> 00:33:09,196
[SPEAKER_02]: Otherwise, there is no movement.

424
00:33:11,608 --> 00:33:16,332
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't have to stop the movement, you just have to recognize its nature, which is not moving.

425
00:33:17,613 --> 00:33:19,675
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, the movement within that is possible.

426
00:33:27,101 --> 00:33:29,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you call it an intention to be a movement?

427
00:33:31,085 --> 00:33:35,108
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, because that, like, if you want to phenomenologically describe it, it would be a movement.

428
00:33:36,265 --> 00:33:36,865
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a movement.

429
00:33:37,426 --> 00:33:43,450
[SPEAKER_02]: Movement of the mind, movement of the speech, movement of the action, of the body, depending how far it takes you.

430
00:33:44,271 --> 00:33:45,251
[SPEAKER_02]: Choice is a movement.

431
00:33:45,992 --> 00:33:46,972
[SPEAKER_02]: It's this or that.

432
00:33:50,435 --> 00:33:58,440
[SPEAKER_00]: And while... because while you... you have chosen some... you choose something and it... and it's... and it's... Well, that only implies the other things that you didn't choose.

433
00:33:58,660 --> 00:33:58,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

434
00:33:59,161 --> 00:34:00,161
[SPEAKER_00]: But while I'm...

435
00:34:00,322 --> 00:34:03,264
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm sitting now, but I'm doing that walk.

436
00:34:03,284 --> 00:34:05,065
[SPEAKER_00]: So my intention to sit is... remains

437
00:34:06,220 --> 00:34:10,245
[SPEAKER_00]: As long as I don't choose something else.

438
00:34:10,265 --> 00:34:11,627
[SPEAKER_02]: They will require you to stand up.

439
00:34:13,829 --> 00:34:18,575
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm constantly... if I wasn't choosing to sit down, I would be doing something else.

440
00:34:19,175 --> 00:34:22,620
[SPEAKER_02]: And now, you have made a choice of sitting down.

441
00:34:23,850 --> 00:34:34,379
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, you're not actively, constantly choosing to remain seated, but the fact that you have made that choice, and your body is still seated, means you're actively engaged in that choice.

442
00:34:34,399 --> 00:34:35,059
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's right.

443
00:34:35,079 --> 00:34:36,961
[SPEAKER_02]: You're engaged, it's active, it's on.

444
00:34:37,261 --> 00:34:37,461
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

445
00:34:38,802 --> 00:34:44,587
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why the four body postures can be a good foundation of mindfulness.

446
00:34:45,007 --> 00:34:50,812
[SPEAKER_02]: Because within that, now, any intentionality that you have will come from the intentionality of the seated body.

447
00:34:50,832 --> 00:34:51,513
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means you can't

448
00:34:52,685 --> 00:34:59,768
[SPEAKER_02]: All the other intentionalities that would exist on account of a different body posture are now absent.

449
00:34:59,848 --> 00:35:08,211
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't, like now, start intending things that would require you to run or stand up without running or standing up.

450
00:35:08,831 --> 00:35:12,652
[SPEAKER_02]: So even if you think about it, that's not really an intention, because it's not really a possibility.

451
00:35:12,712 --> 00:35:18,214
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just an abstract thought that you think from the point of view of seated intentions.

452
00:35:18,375 --> 00:35:19,415
[SPEAKER_02]: Body in this posture

453
00:35:21,295 --> 00:35:29,997
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, so what's the duty then?

454
00:35:30,618 --> 00:35:37,800
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I'm watching my mind... Well, the duty is, you don't really have to be analyzing the domains of your intention and what's given and what's not.

455
00:35:37,900 --> 00:35:50,363
[SPEAKER_02]: The duty is to see that whenever the movement is possible, whenever the movement is occurring, intention, volition, it's only because things in regard to which you're moving are given beforehand.

456
00:35:52,278 --> 00:35:53,299
[SPEAKER_00]: As a more general thing.

457
00:35:54,119 --> 00:35:55,821
[SPEAKER_00]: I've seen impermanence.

458
00:35:55,901 --> 00:35:56,321
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

459
00:35:56,341 --> 00:36:05,107
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, any movement that occurs, that will occur, that has occurred, is within, is now seen within that inherent impermanence.

460
00:36:06,468 --> 00:36:16,415
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, you do that long enough, any movement, no matter how quick, outleeting or whatever else, will not be taken as mine.

461
00:36:16,755 --> 00:36:16,975
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

462
00:36:17,036 --> 00:36:18,877
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's impossible to be taken as mine.

463
00:36:20,760 --> 00:36:29,808
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you dwell on it long enough, and then it becomes apparent that there is no movement without a still thing being given beforehand, for which, just the fact it was given beforehand, you had no say in its origin.

464
00:36:29,828 --> 00:36:32,430
[SPEAKER_00]: To be able to make a choice, you must have things to choose.

465
00:36:32,630 --> 00:36:33,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

466
00:36:33,711 --> 00:36:34,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Options.

467
00:36:34,892 --> 00:36:35,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah.

468
00:36:36,213 --> 00:36:47,443
[SPEAKER_02]: And those... Options would be more particular, like proliferation, but just the fact that to be able to make choice, you have to be able to move mentally.

469
00:36:48,769 --> 00:36:52,432
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, in order to move mentally, the mind needs to be there beforehand.

470
00:36:53,954 --> 00:37:04,324
[SPEAKER_02]: So, instead of chasing the movement, you actually use movement like as an indication of the fact that the mind is still.

471
00:37:10,690 --> 00:37:13,212
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence the sign of the mind, cittanimitta.

472
00:37:31,913 --> 00:37:40,399
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, long enough there, it uproots craving from... Well, craving is possible for as long as there is sense of I am.

473
00:37:42,961 --> 00:37:46,584
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, I am intending this means, basically, I'm craving this.

474
00:37:47,064 --> 00:38:00,314
[SPEAKER_02]: But once you start realizing that that's completely redundant, and that actually you can be intending and moving as a result of the, you know, five aggregates doing just that, on their own, not requiring you,

475
00:38:02,263 --> 00:38:05,427
[SPEAKER_02]: Then you won't.

476
00:38:05,467 --> 00:38:07,971
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't be needing to crave anymore, in any words.

477
00:38:42,375 --> 00:38:46,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I suppose the question comes up many times.

478
00:38:47,418 --> 00:38:55,603
[SPEAKER_00]: If I'm not the one intending, then who's going to be responsible for the choices?

479
00:38:55,663 --> 00:38:56,303
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's the thing.

480
00:38:56,503 --> 00:39:04,188
[SPEAKER_02]: The question who implies someone and the implication of someone means, well, the sense of self is still there.

481
00:39:04,868 --> 00:39:10,912
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whether you have that sense of self as internally or externally or both, it's still there.

482
00:39:12,075 --> 00:39:13,777
[SPEAKER_02]: So, ah, well, if it's not me, who is it?

483
00:39:13,897 --> 00:39:20,062
[SPEAKER_02]: Means, well, the general, then the nature of the self-view is still there, because now you're looking for somebody else.

484
00:39:22,204 --> 00:39:36,718
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you start seeing that even that movement, that looking, that intending, that thinking, is possible only because thing is given before, and you just start including everything properly, and if you do long enough, then it will sort of...

485
00:39:38,599 --> 00:39:42,781
[SPEAKER_02]: You cancel out the things that were never meant to be there in the first place.

486
00:39:43,441 --> 00:39:45,422
[SPEAKER_02]: Your sense of self, craving, and all that.

487
00:39:45,442 --> 00:39:47,343
[SPEAKER_02]: Because none of that is required.

488
00:39:47,503 --> 00:39:48,824
[SPEAKER_02]: It's completely redundant.

489
00:39:48,884 --> 00:39:50,845
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why we refer to it as a parasite.

490
00:39:51,626 --> 00:39:54,987
[SPEAKER_02]: Upon... gratuitous parasite, but completely beginningless.

491
00:39:56,008 --> 00:39:59,409
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's no first point that ignorance has manifested before which you were not ignorant.

492
00:40:00,230 --> 00:40:02,271
[SPEAKER_02]: But actually, ignorance is not supposed to be there.

493
00:40:02,791 --> 00:40:03,471
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not required.

494
00:40:04,512 --> 00:40:06,473
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, when you know that, you're not ignorant.

495
00:40:06,983 --> 00:40:09,624
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no, no, no, no, no, no space in between.

496
00:40:10,165 --> 00:40:11,865
[SPEAKER_02]: Either you're ignorant or you're not ignorant.

497
00:40:14,267 --> 00:40:17,788
[SPEAKER_02]: Knowing that ignorance has no room, removes ignorance.

498
00:40:20,890 --> 00:40:22,010
[SPEAKER_02]: It's mutually exclusive.

499
00:40:22,030 --> 00:40:30,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Vijaya and avijaya, you can't... Knowing that you're seeing things in the wrong way.

500
00:40:30,655 --> 00:40:35,037
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, knowing, for example, that you can't own things in the first place.

501
00:40:35,848 --> 00:40:41,472
[SPEAKER_02]: Not knowing that makes you own them, through that not knowing.

502
00:40:41,492 --> 00:40:44,795
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence a vijja is responsible for your sense of self, for your appropriation.

503
00:40:45,535 --> 00:40:55,603
[SPEAKER_02]: Knowing fully and discerning fully that you can't actually own them, means vijja is responsible for your disowning and complete removal of appropriation.

504
00:40:58,445 --> 00:40:59,066
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the thing.

505
00:40:59,146 --> 00:41:04,850
[SPEAKER_02]: You will know that you can't own them and you see them fully determined always by that which is arisen beforehand.

506
00:41:06,694 --> 00:41:07,615
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not saying that.

507
00:41:07,935 --> 00:41:09,936
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just a reason.

508
00:41:09,956 --> 00:41:14,539
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever arises will be determined by this, that has arisen beforehand.

509
00:41:14,839 --> 00:41:18,041
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I can't even conceive to own it.

510
00:41:18,642 --> 00:41:24,145
[SPEAKER_02]: The only way you can conceive to own it is when you forget that it's determined by that which has arisen beforehand.

511
00:41:25,666 --> 00:41:27,507
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's not tricky.

512
00:41:29,869 --> 00:41:31,830
[SPEAKER_02]: And the practice is in discerning.

513
00:41:31,890 --> 00:41:35,372
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the whole pathicinam part is discerning.

514
00:41:36,980 --> 00:41:39,883
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, discerning the connection between the two.

515
00:41:39,923 --> 00:41:48,571
[SPEAKER_02]: And the connection is that connection of dependence, determination, being determined by, being inseparable from, saṅkhara.

516
00:42:00,902 --> 00:42:02,324
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's not, there is nothing else.

517
00:42:03,525 --> 00:42:10,766
[SPEAKER_02]: In your experience, then, the five aggregates, whether put it in another hand, whatever you've been experiencing or not, it's just the five aggregates, one way or the other.

518
00:42:14,327 --> 00:42:17,107
[SPEAKER_02]: But you want to discern the way they arise.

519
00:42:19,328 --> 00:42:25,389
[SPEAKER_02]: So, within that which you already have, which you've been experiencing, within that, you need to start discerning the right things.

520
00:42:26,029 --> 00:42:30,750
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the the correct instruction is absolutely crucial.

521
00:42:32,310 --> 00:42:43,777
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you can have all the right attention, proper ayoniso, manasikāra, proper attention in your life, if there is nobody telling you what you need to attend properly, how, you simply won't do it.

522
00:42:43,817 --> 00:42:44,557
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't know how.

523
00:42:45,618 --> 00:42:48,659
[SPEAKER_02]: Because there is no immediate confirmation whether it's right or wrong.

524
00:42:50,540 --> 00:42:57,685
[SPEAKER_02]: Only after repeating it for five hundred thousands of times, then the confirmation, ah, okay, yes, I see this.

525
00:42:58,405 --> 00:42:59,506
[SPEAKER_02]: I see the direct connection.

526
00:42:59,986 --> 00:43:01,787
[SPEAKER_02]: But you would be repeating something,

527
00:43:03,353 --> 00:43:08,117
[SPEAKER_02]: Five hundred or thousands of times, that doesn't bear any fruit initially.

528
00:43:08,157 --> 00:43:12,801
[SPEAKER_02]: Only if somebody tells you that you should keep repeating it, that eventually will show you the fruit.

529
00:43:12,821 --> 00:43:16,604
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you can't stumble upon dhamma accidentally.

530
00:43:16,624 --> 00:43:18,425
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't accidentally become a sotāpanna.

531
00:43:19,466 --> 00:43:20,387
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, you had all the luck.

532
00:43:20,427 --> 00:43:22,349
[SPEAKER_02]: You just thought the right thing at the right time.

533
00:43:22,509 --> 00:43:23,009
[SPEAKER_02]: Impossible.

534
00:43:23,830 --> 00:43:26,272
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to think the right thing at the right time for thousands of times.

535
00:43:26,332 --> 00:43:28,694
[SPEAKER_02]: Just repetition, repetition, repetition, repetition.

536
00:43:29,570 --> 00:43:31,850
[SPEAKER_02]: Until it wears out, the ignorance.

537
00:43:32,410 --> 00:43:36,271
[SPEAKER_02]: When it's worn out sufficiently, for you to see over it...

538
00:43:36,751 --> 00:43:40,872
[SPEAKER_02]: Imagine, again, like tall trees, you can't see anything from, whatever.

539
00:43:41,372 --> 00:43:44,112
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you're just hacking away, and hacking away, and hacking away.

540
00:43:44,473 --> 00:43:46,793
[SPEAKER_02]: And you still don't see anything, because it's tall trees still.

541
00:43:47,213 --> 00:43:48,293
[SPEAKER_02]: Tall bushes, tall everything.

542
00:43:49,133 --> 00:43:52,254
[SPEAKER_02]: But when you have hacked away sufficiently, you start to see.

543
00:43:53,474 --> 00:43:58,595
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you know, oh, this hacking is actually working, because now I have a degree of confirmation.

544
00:43:59,625 --> 00:44:00,365
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's sotāpaṭṭhāna.

545
00:44:00,785 --> 00:44:09,749
[SPEAKER_02]: You keep hacking further, now you know for sure, you have it confirmed for yourself that this hacking works in such and such way, and you keep doing it, and then eventually you'll see enough.

546
00:44:11,270 --> 00:44:12,310
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll see all you need to see.

547
00:44:26,908 --> 00:44:27,929
[SPEAKER_02]: But if nobody tells you...

548
00:44:27,949 --> 00:44:29,730
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you can stop and think for yourself.

549
00:44:30,111 --> 00:44:48,465
[SPEAKER_02]: If nobody tells you that repeating something, a way of attending things, a way of reflecting, if that is the right thing, and if you don't have faith in what they told you is the right thing, I mean, what are the chances of you, yourself, doing something right, that you don't know is right,

550
00:44:48,947 --> 00:44:54,528
[SPEAKER_02]: And then doing exactly the same thing on and on and on and on for no apparent reason.

551
00:44:54,548 --> 00:44:55,809
[SPEAKER_02]: For absolutely no apparent reason.

552
00:44:55,829 --> 00:44:58,569
[SPEAKER_02]: Because nobody told you externally you should be doing nothing.

553
00:44:59,650 --> 00:45:02,790
[SPEAKER_02]: While everything, your whole experience is directed completely the opposite way.

554
00:45:05,291 --> 00:45:05,911
[SPEAKER_02]: It's impossible.

555
00:45:06,131 --> 00:45:07,251
[SPEAKER_02]: It's literally impossible.

556
00:45:07,751 --> 00:45:11,732
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's impossible for somebody to arrive at the right view just by accident.

557
00:45:13,313 --> 00:45:16,274
